Three pragmatic deviations from the prompt's literal wording were
made and are documented inline in the up.sql header. None of them
change the names of objects (the gate's substring check sees every
required name as required) or the SI-canonical unit choice that
ADR-004 mandates:

  D1. `cagg_fleet_stats` is created as a regular MATERIALIZED VIEW
      sourced from `drives`, not as a TimescaleDB continuous aggregate
      sourced from `positions`. Reasons: (a) the legacy contract that
      every in-tree consumer queries (energy_repo.go .GetDailyBreakdown
      / .GetTotals; regen_handler.go .regenSummary;
      maintenance_worker.go .refreshFleetStats) is a per-(vehicle_id,
      day) drive roll-up; (b) `drives` is a non-hypertable per
      000172_drives_si, so a real continuous aggregate over `drives` is
      not available; (c) silently changing the source under the same
      object name would corrupt the meaning of `drive_count`,
      `total_distance`, `total_energy`, etc.

  D2. `cagg_charging_summary` is created as a regular MATERIALIZED
      VIEW. `charging_sessions` is a non-hypertable per
      000171_charging_si, so a continuous aggregate over it is not
      available. The cagg_ name prefix is preserved per the prompt's
      gate substring check; the in-tree precedent for naming a regular
      MV with the cagg_ prefix is the legacy `cagg_fleet_stats` from
      000142_baseline_typed (its COMMENT explicitly called out the
      same naming compromise).

  D3. `cagg_vehicle_daily` is sourced from `positions` only, not from
      `positions + drives`. A single continuous aggregate cannot span
      two tables — the cagg invalidation machinery hooks the source
      hypertable's chunks, and mutations to `drives` would not
      invalidate the cagg. drive_count and energy_wh are deferred to
      read-time JOIN with `cagg_fleet_stats` or `drives`.

The four real continuous aggregates (cagg_battery_daily,
cagg_climate_hourly, cagg_signal_hourly, cagg_vehicle_daily) each get
an `add_continuous_aggregate_policy` registration. The five regular
materialized views (cagg_fleet_stats, cagg_charging_summary,
mv_energy_daily, mv_position_hourly, mv_signal_stats) get UNIQUE
INDEXES so REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY works, and their
refresh schedule is documented under === REFRESH_POLICIES === for ops
to wire up via maintenance_worker / pg_cron / Helm CronJob (the
existing maintenance_worker already refreshes cagg_fleet_stats every
hour).

A pre-flight rubber-duck pass identified four blocking issues with an
earlier draft of this design:
  - `cagg_battery_daily` proposed columns broke the existing trend
    queries in battery_handler.go and analytics_handler_queries.go
    (which expect `bucket`, `end_soc`, `min_soc`, `max_soc`).
    Resolved: those four column NAMES are preserved. `end_soc` is
    last(float_value, ts) FILTER (WHERE field='BatteryLevel').
  - `cagg_signal_hourly` proposed columns broke
    signal_history_writer.go .Stats (which expects `signal_name`,
    `sample_count`, `min_value`, `max_value`, `avg_value`).
    Resolved: `field AS signal_name` aliasing preserves the legacy
    column. min/max/avg are rolled up over `float_value` (the
    ValueKindFloat-typed column that covers all numeric-stats
    consumers).
  - `cagg_fleet_stats` proposed sourcing from `positions` broke
    energy_repo.go and regen_handler.go which query drive-derived
    columns. Resolved per D1 above (regular MV sourced from `drives`).
  - `mv_position_hourly` proposed using `array_agg(... ORDER BY ts)`
    for start/end lat/lng. Resolved: replaced with TimescaleDB
    `first(lat, ts)` / `last(lat, ts)` (cheaper, semantically clearer,
    and TimescaleDB is already required by every cagg in this file).
The feedback also flagged that creating regular MVs `WITH NO DATA`
breaks the first `REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY` call.
Resolved: every regular MV in this file is created in the default
WITH DATA mode (zero rows initially because the source tables are
empty in a fresh database, but the MV is "populated" so subsequent
CONCURRENTLY refreshes work without a non-concurrent first refresh).
This was verified end-to-end against a TimescaleDB container under
=== APPLIED === below.

=== ROLLUPS ===
The migration creates nine rollups in the order they appear in the
up.sql, each with the role and source the prompt specifies, modulo
the deviations documented in === HONESTY_COVENANT === above.

  1. cagg_battery_daily         CAGG over signal_log
       per (vehicle_id, day): bucket / vehicle_id / min_soc / max_soc /
         avg_soc / end_soc / start_soc / soc_sample_count /
         ac_energy_added_wh / dc_energy_added_wh / energy_consumed_wh
       fields read: BatteryLevel (SoC), ACChargingEnergyIn,
         DCChargingEnergyIn, EnergyRemaining
         (all four are ValueKindFloat per
         internal/tesla/protomodel/signal_metadata_gen.go, so the
         rollup reads from float_value)
       refresh policy: 30d start_offset / 1h end_offset / 1h schedule
       legacy contract preserved: bucket, end_soc, min_soc, max_soc
         (battery_handler.go:146, analytics_handler_queries.go:267)

  2. cagg_climate_hourly        CAGG over climate_snapshots
       per (vehicle_id, hour): bucket / vehicle_id / avg_inside_temp_c /
         avg_outside_temp_c / min/max inside/outside temps /
         hvac_active_sample_count / sample_count
       refresh policy: 7d start_offset / 1h end_offset / 30m schedule
       hvac_active_minutes interpretation: the prompt asks for active
         minutes, but Fleet Telemetry's HVAC sampling cadence varies,
         so the rollup exposes the COUNT of samples with hvac_power=TRUE
         within the hour and lets the consumer multiply by the known
         cadence rather than committing to a fixed minute conversion
         at the rollup layer.

  3. cagg_signal_hourly         CAGG over signal_log
       per (vehicle_id, hour, field): hour / vehicle_id / signal_name
         (= field) / sample_count / min_value / max_value / avg_value /
         last_float_value / last_int_value / last_bool_value /
         last_str_value
       refresh policy: 30d start_offset / 1h end_offset / 1h schedule
       legacy contract preserved: signal_name, sample_count, min_value,
         max_value, avg_value (signal_history_writer.go:189-193)

  4. cagg_fleet_stats           REGULAR MV over drives          (D1)
       per (vehicle_id, day): vehicle_id / day / drive_count /
         total_distance_m / total_energy_wh / total_regen_wh /
         total_duration_s / avg_speed_mps / max_speed_mps /
         avg_start_soc_pct / avg_end_soc_pct / avg_ambient_temp_c
       UNIQUE INDEX cagg_fleet_stats_pk (vehicle_id, day) for
         REFRESH CONCURRENTLY support
       refresh: maintenance_worker.go .refreshFleetStats already
         refreshes this MV CONCURRENTLY every maintenance cycle
       SI columns (m, Wh, s, mps); legacy mi/kwh/mph consumers
         (energy_repo.go, regen_handler.go) need adapter changes in a
         later phase-42 prompt.

  5. cagg_vehicle_daily         CAGG over positions             (D3)
       per (vehicle_id, day): bucket / vehicle_id / odometer_max_m /
         odometer_min_m / distance_m (= max - min odometer) /
         avg_speed_mps / max_speed_mps / active_sample_count /
         sample_count
       refresh policy: 30d start_offset / 1h end_offset / 1h schedule
       drive_count and energy_wh are deferred to read-time JOIN with
         cagg_fleet_stats or drives (cagg invalidation cannot span two
         tables).

  6. cagg_charging_summary      REGULAR MV over charging_sessions (D2)
       per (vehicle_id, day): vehicle_id / day / sessions_count /
         total_energy_wh / avg_peak_power_w / max_peak_power_w /
         avg_power_w / avg_delta_soc_pct / total_duration_s /
         avg_start_soc_pct / avg_end_soc_pct
       UNIQUE INDEX cagg_charging_summary_pk (vehicle_id, day) for
         REFRESH CONCURRENTLY support
       refresh: ops-scheduled (see === REFRESH_POLICIES === below)
       SI columns (Wh, W, s).

  7. mv_energy_daily            REGULAR MV (FULL OUTER JOIN)
       FULL OUTER JOIN of charging_sessions and drives daily aggregates
       per (vehicle_id, day): vehicle_id / day / charging_energy_wh /
         drive_energy_wh / regen_energy_wh / net_energy_wh
         (= charging - drive) / sessions_count / drives_count /
         distance_m / duration_s
       UNIQUE INDEX mv_energy_daily_pk (vehicle_id, day) for
         REFRESH CONCURRENTLY support
       refresh: ops-scheduled (see === REFRESH_POLICIES === below)

  8. mv_position_hourly         REGULAR MV over positions
       per (vehicle_id, hour): vehicle_id / hour / start_lat /
         start_lng / end_lat / end_lng / avg_speed_mps / max_speed_mps /
         avg_altitude_m / start_odometer_m / end_odometer_m /
         sample_count
       start_*/end_* via TimescaleDB first(value, ts) / last(value, ts)
       UNIQUE INDEX mv_position_hourly_pk (vehicle_id, hour)
       refresh: ops-scheduled (see === REFRESH_POLICIES === below)

  9. mv_signal_stats            REGULAR MV over signal_log
       per (field, day): field / day / row_count / str_count /
         bool_count / int_count / float_count / time_count /
         distinct_vehicles / fill_ratio (= share of rows with any
         typed-column non-null, rounded to 4 decimals)
       UNIQUE INDEX mv_signal_stats_pk (field, day)
       refresh: ops-scheduled (see === REFRESH_POLICIES === below)

Total objects: 9 rollups + 5 unique indexes (one per regular MV) +
4 add_continuous_aggregate_policy registrations + 9 COMMENTs.

up.sql line count: 508 lines (heavily commented per honesty covenant).
down.sql line count: 21 lines.

